# Getting started with TADp

## Audience

This is the complete portable procedure for installing TADp and operating it
in a new or existing project. It is explanatory; the
[governing suite](../../evidence/authoritative/LLM/00_Codex_Invariant_Suite_Index.md)
owns requirements. Examples are reference-only: they create no actual project,
owner appointment, decision, adoption, acceptance, execution or verification.

## Required software and access

Use **Git and Codex**, with access to project instructions, evidence, Git state,
human decision makers and permitted edits/checks. Python 3 runs bundled checks;
synchronization requires Python 3.11 or later. Other tools need a recorded
[compatibility assessment](adapting-other-tools.md).

Select installation root `P` explicitly and discover enclosing Git root `G`.
P can equal G or be nested, such as `G/projects/widget`. A nested installation
uses the enclosing Git history and needs no nested repository. Resolve paths,
reject escaping links/junctions and preserve parent/sibling instructions and
files. Git identity does not broaden project authority. See
[specification 05](../../evidence/authoritative/LLM/05_Codex_Git_Workflow_Specification.md#requirement).

## Choose an adoption path

Installation, adoption and migration are distinct events. Existing projects
retain recorded compatible revisions and claims until explicitly migrated.
Every TADp task applies the
[keyword contract](../../evidence/authoritative/LLM/11_Codex_Prompt_Keyword_Specification.md);
reading its examples activates no directive or gate exception.

### Use this repository as the TADp reference implementation

Compatibility entry point: maintaining a protocol source repository follows
that repository's contributor documentation and expressly authorized
candidate/delivery boundaries. This project-use guide grants no generic
maintenance authority. Continue below to install TADp in a consuming project.

### Adopt TADp in an existing product repository

Inspect existing instructions, Git state, actual branches, integration
configuration, hooks and selected P contents first. Pin the actual immutable
distribution commit and inspect its component set. Review collisions before
copying; reconcile instructions without weakening either set and preserve
unrelated files. Keep recovery bytes outside the intended overwrite set.

For a new empty destination the following manual mapping applies. Existing
destinations need file-by-file reconciliation; these examples are not a merge
algorithm. `SOURCE` is the inspected distribution directory at that pinned
commit, wherever obtained, not a development project's populated records.

| Source relative to SOURCE | Destination relative to P | Handling |
|---|---|---|
| `template/` (complete) | `template/` | Preserve every file, scoped AGENTS.md, workflow prompts and outer .tadp-lock.json; do not specialize |
| Contents of `template/project/` | `.` | Include dotfiles, .githooks/, .gitattributes, project AGENTS.md, prompts/AGENTS.md and prompts/README.md, and .template.md sources |
| `template/project/template/README.md` | Remains inside the copied library | Nonrecursive placeholder; do not overwrite the complete installed template/README.md with it |
| `template/project/evidence/authoritative/LLM/` | `evidence/authoritative/LLM/` (already in scaffold contents) | Keep the complete embedded suite, scoped guard and matching .tadp-lock.json unchanged; do not overlay a different suite |
| `docs/` | `docs/` | Portable adoption, operating and migration guides |
| `tools/` | `tools/` | Protection, validation and all imported runtime modules and tool documentation |
| `tests/` | `tests/` | Applicable distribution regressions and fixtures; a scaffold's tests/README alone is not coverage |

Copy scaffold contents first except its placeholder `template/` directory;
then the complete outer library and other mapped components. The embedded
authoritative suite is installed by the scaffold copy. Do not union it with
the distribution's separately locked suite: even a leftover README changes
the protected file set. An older source without a complete embedded suite
needs its own reviewed exact assembly plan. Select sources
at one coherent pinned revision. Keep licenses and provenance. Specialize
working record copies outside protected sources. No populated profile,
decision, realization, initiative, product or test outcome is inherited.

The inspected synchronization tool supports existing selected targets;
consult [the tool reference](../../tools/README.md). Its default authority is
the local locked template; remote refresh is explicit. It does not install the
distribution's full regression tree. Manual copying here is the demonstrated
complete documentation/regression assembly; another command need not install
the same payload. Never refresh locks to conceal unexplained drift. Existing
unlocked installations use [integrity migration](../guides/protected-source-integrity-migration.md).

### Start a new repository

Initialize Git in G and retain an initial baseline commit before initiative
work. Select P within that worktree, then follow the same component map and
adoption process. Discover actual branch names rather than assuming main/master
or a hosting provider.

## Worked installation example

Reference-only PowerShell for an empty P. Replace placeholders before running.
`$TadpPython` is the actual configured interpreter returned by environment
tooling. `$Source` is an already inspected local distribution at the verified
commit. No network, hook activation or protected-source revision is implied.

```powershell
# Working directory: inspected source Git worktree, outside the target.
$DistributionCommit = '<actual immutable commit>'
git rev-parse HEAD
# Compare that output to DistributionCommit; inspect status before copying.
$Source = '<absolute distribution directory at that commit>'
$GitRoot = '<absolute target Git worktree>'
$ProjectRoot = $GitRoot   # Root installation: P = G
# Nested variant instead: $ProjectRoot = Join-Path $GitRoot 'projects/widget'
$TadpPython = '<configured Python executable>'

# Working directory: target G. NEW project only, if not already in Git:
# git init
# Create/review your initial files; stage and commit the selected baseline.
# Existing projects retain history; do not initialize nested Git inside P.
Set-Location -LiteralPath $GitRoot
git rev-parse --show-toplevel
git status --short --branch
git config --show-origin --get core.hooksPath
# No configured value makes that command exit 1; inspect default hooks too.
New-Item -ItemType Directory -Force -Path $ProjectRoot | Out-Null

# Only after collision review establishes an EMPTY destination:
Get-ChildItem -LiteralPath (Join-Path $Source 'template/project') -Force |
    Where-Object Name -ne 'template' |
    Copy-Item -Destination $ProjectRoot -Recurse -Force
Copy-Item -LiteralPath (Join-Path $Source 'template') -Destination $ProjectRoot -Recurse
# The complete embedded authoritative suite and its lock are already copied.
# Do not overlay the separately locked distribution suite.
foreach ($Component in @('docs', 'tools', 'tests')) {
    New-Item -ItemType Directory -Force -Path (Join-Path $ProjectRoot $Component) | Out-Null
    Get-ChildItem -LiteralPath (Join-Path $Source $Component) -Force |
        Copy-Item -Destination (Join-Path $ProjectRoot $Component) -Recurse -Force
}

# Working directory: selected P, not an inferred Git root.
Set-Location -LiteralPath $ProjectRoot
& $TadpPython tools/tadp_protect.py check .
& $TadpPython tools/tadp_validate.py .
& $TadpPython -m unittest discover -s tests
```

These commands include dotfiles and copy contents into existing component
directories, avoiding extra `docs/docs` or `tools/tools` levels. For populated
P plan individual merges instead. Retain source identity and hashes in actual
installation evidence; the example's placeholders are not a provenance record.

## Project layout and ownership

```text
G/                                  actual Git root
`-- [optional project prefix]/      P; empty prefix means P = G
    |-- AGENTS.md and README.md
    |-- project-profile.md          actual ownership and adoption
    |-- evidence/evidence-register.md
    |-- evidence/authoritative/LLM/ guarded suite and lock
    |-- evidence/non-authoritative/realizations/
    |-- initiatives/
    |   |-- version-scopes.md, initiative-index.md, status.md
    |   |-- version-scope-migration-ledger.md
    |   |-- version-correlation-migration-ledger.md
    |   |-- work-conflicts.md
    |   `-- <scope>/<type>/<id-and-slug>/
    |-- products/                   records link actual deliverables
    |-- prompts/                    project-local instructions
    |-- template/                   protected library and lock
    |   `-- prompts/                protected stages 0 through 13
    `-- docs/, tools/, tests/, .githooks/
```

Create live records from their canonical sources only when triggered; the tree
does not invent events. The [coverage map](../../template/TEMPLATE-COVERAGE.md)
selects profiles, evidence registers, scope/membership/status records, ledgers,
products and decisions. Record actual human owners and approval roles; no
source-repository appointment is inherited. Evidence sources retain their
named authorities. See [project layout](repository-layout.md).

Project-local `prompts/` preserves its installed guard. It is distinct from
protected `template/prompts/` and any source repository's maintenance prompts.
Copying or changing cwd confers no generic maintenance/outside-project authority.

## Adoption and migration

Record the distribution commit, installed baseline, actual Git root/prefix,
project boundary, owners, integration conventions and authority in the profile
and linked evidence. Obtain explicit human prospective adoption of the actual
governing commit; capture the pre-adoption coherence baseline separately.
Establish applicable ledgers and coordination records before scope allocation.
Give every legacy record a complete Unmigrated, Deferred or separately
authorized migration disposition. Preserve prior claims, revisions, sources,
branches and paths. Installation and adoption accept no initiative, change no
lifecycle, and release no product.

Consult [installation-root adoption](../guides/project-root-installation-migration.md),
[scope operation](../guides/version-scope-migration.md),
[correlation migration](../guides/version-realization-correlation-migration.md),
[coherence adoption](../guides/initiative-coherence-migration.md),
[keyword adoption](../guides/prompt-keywords-migration.md) and
[local prompt migration](../guides/project-local-prompts-migration.md) as applicable.

For the worked example, the following record sketch is reference-only. Replace
each value from actual evidence and use the canonical profile/decision sources;
the sketch supplies no human response or live adoption event.

| Example record | Illustrative content to obtain and record |
|---|---|
| `project-profile.md`: roots | P=`<selected project directory>`; G=`<Git-reported root>`; prefix=`<empty or projects/widget>` |
| Profile: source | Governing revision=`<verified immutable distribution commit>`; installation evidence=`<component map and actual hashes>` |
| Profile: people and Git | Owner/approval authority=`<actual human appointment or delegation>`; integration base=`<discovered and coordinated branch>` |
| Adoption receipt | Authority=`<actual human responder and role>`; exact instruction=`<actual prospective adoption response>`; effective event=`<actual event/date>` |
| Coherence baseline | `<actual pre-adoption Git commit>`, retained separately from the distribution source revision |
| Migration ledgers | `<each actual legacy record>` -> `Unmigrated`, `Deferred` or explicitly authorized migration with its evidence; new projects record the actual empty legacy inventory |

For example, a nested installation's prefix could be `projects/widget` while
its distribution commit identifies an entirely different source repository.
The project retains its own Git baseline and appointments. Create and reconcile
these records only after their real decisions/evidence exist; none accepts the
first pilot initiative.

## First validation

Run the checks from P using the configured interpreter, against that exact
installation, pinned locks, Git history and actual records. Protection should
exit 0 with zero errors. Structural validation exits 0 without errors, 1 with
errors, or 2 for invalid inputs; warnings remain visible. Regressions report
actual pass/fail/skip counts. Run project-specific product checks separately,
recording commands/results against the pilot's accepted claim. Structural
checks prove neither human acceptance nor product correctness. Tests cannot
supply missing facts, authority or actual work.

Investigate failures before allocating governed work. Compare with the pinned
baseline and distinguish inherited findings. Never disable checks or refresh
locks to hide drift. See [validation and quality](validation-and-quality.md).

## Git and hooks

Discover branches, shared Git configuration and existing default/custom hooks
at G. Coordinate dispatch manually before explicit activation. Root P uses
Git-relative `.githooks`; nested `projects/widget` uses
`projects/widget/.githooks`. An explicitly authorized
`git -C <G> config core.hooksPath <verified-relative-hook-path>` changes shared
configuration; copying files does not activate it. Set `TADP_PYTHON` to the
configured executable if needed. See [Git and Codex](git-and-codex-requirements.md).

## First initiative

Choose one narrow pilot outcome, owner, authority boundary and acceptance claim.
Capture non-authoritative realization notes with precise provenance; a human
intake instruction delegates adopted fit/routing, not acceptance. Use the
[numbered workflow](../../template/prompts/README.md) in order: 0 realization
fit/preparation; 1 proposal; 2 proposal alignment; 3 initial suite; 4 initiative
alignment; 5 human assumption review; 6 human question review; 7 explicit human
acceptance of the exact baseline. Retain answers in linked receipts. Apply
[prerequisite recovery](../guides/template-prerequisites-migration.md) without
inventing missing decisions or identity.

After acceptance follow exactly one declared path: implementation 8 through 10
(including separately read-only readiness), design 11, or planning 12.
Implementation changes products; design changes its declared specifications;
planning creates initiative-local evidence. Completion requires type-specific
execution/delivery records, exact verification, coherence feedback and completion
evidence. Stage 13 finalizes a truthfully completed initiative and creates no
acceptance/completion by itself. See [initiative operation](initiative-workflow.md).

## Before inviting contributors

Record integration and dedicated/shared scope execution assignments, captured
bases, initiative-specific commits and verification. Coordinate shared Git
state and preserve unrelated work. Include proposed/partial work in coherence
reviews, record conflicts and require authorized reopening before refining
closed claims. See [scope operation](../guides/version-scope-migration.md) and
[coherence guidance](../guides/initiative-coherence-migration.md).

Confirm approval roles, evidence owners, protected-source review and product
checks. Local child-to-parent integration needs its identified close/finalize
authority and passed gates. Further integration, remote writes, publication
and release retain separate authority. A pilot does not silently migrate other work.
